\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}

\looseness=-2 We showed that learned post-hoc correction of frozen flow-matching VLAs is harmful by default. Under a K-matched design ($K{=}2$, same $\varepsilon$ and CRM checkpoint), unconditional correction degrades all four LIBERO suites on SmolVLA (0.4B, up to $-$14.8\,pp on Long) and all four suites on $\pi_{0.5}$ (3.6B, up to $-$17.0\,pp on Long; preliminary single-seed). The root cause is that 80--97\% of control steps are already confident; corrections on these steps inject noise. Calibrated confidence sets from $K{=}2$ pairwise $L_2$ distances detect confident steps and gate the CRM, recovering nearly all degradation ($\Delta_{\mathrm{gate}}{=}{+}2.8$ to $+$14.5\,pp). This is a diagnostic contribution: when a practitioner deploys a learned correction module (e.g., from demonstrations or a third party), calibrated gating prevents degradation on the majority of confident steps; if no correction module is deployed, VLA-only remains the simpler choice. The key finding is that \emph{knowing when not to correct} matters more than correction strength.

\section{Limitations}
\label{sec:limitations}

\looseness=-1 \textbf{Effect sizes and statistical power.}\enspace Paired improvements ($\tau$-gated vs.\ VLA-only) reach $p{=}0.017$ on Spatial ($n{=}8$; Appendix~\ref{sec:8seed}) but do not reach significance on other suites with $n{=}3$. The primary claim is $\tau$-gated vs.\ CRM always-on under the K-matched design: calibrated gating exceeds unconditional correction on all four suites ($\Delta_{\mathrm{gate}}{=}{+}2.8$ to $+$14.5\,pp), with Long providing the clearest signal ($\Delta_{\mathrm{gate}}{=}{+}14.5$\,pp).

\looseness=-1 \textbf{Confidently wrong steps.}\enspace The pairwise $L_2$ probe measures sample disagreement, not correctness. On Long (trigger rate ${\sim}$3\%), the VLA may produce confident but systematically incorrect outputs that escape gating.

\looseness=-1 \textbf{Partial recovery on stronger models.}\enspace While $\tau$-gating nearly fully recovers CRM-induced degradation on SmolVLA, recovery on $\pi_{0.5}$ is partial: gating recovers roughly half of the degradation on Goal and Long (Appendix, Table~\ref{tab:tau_sweep_pi05}), suggesting that gating effectiveness depends on the quality of the underlying CRM corrections, which diminishes when the base policy is already near-expert.

\looseness=-1 \textbf{CRM design space.}\enspace Our finding that unconditional correction degrades performance is established for the tested lightweight MLP-based CRM (327K parameters, $L_2$ loss, offline training). Whether larger architectures, online fine-tuning, or alternative training objectives would change this conclusion remains open. The degradation mechanisms differ between in-distribution suites (Spatial, Object, Goal; where the CRM is trained) and the OOD suite (Long; excluded from CRM training): in-distribution degradation reflects noise injection on confident steps, while OOD degradation additionally reflects unreliable correction predictions.

\looseness=-1 \textbf{Scope and generalization.}\enspace The CRM requires task-specific calibration data (10 demos per task); deterministic VLAs lie outside scope (Appendix~\ref{sec:applicability}). The threshold $\tau$ controls a precision--recall tradeoff: optimal $\tau$ is suite-dependent (Appendix~\ref{sec:tau_sweep}), motivating adaptive $\tau$ selection as future work. All experiments use LIBERO simulation; real-robot transfer remains future work. Broader cross-architecture validation is needed.

